  "query_preview": "..\nKEY MESSAGE 1...\n\nLatest trends in guidance indicating showers developing a bit \nearlier than previously forecast, as a lead disturbance \napproaching from the Great Lakes moves in. Will mention likely \nto categorical PoPs developing from NW to SE for areas mainly \nalong and north of I-90 later this afternoon to early evening. \nHumidity levels will noticeably increase today, with dewpoints \nrising into the 60s. Showers with some embedded convective \ndownpours are expected to then spread south/east across the rest\nof the area later this evening into tonight ahead of a cold \nfront. Latest guidance (including REFS/HREF) still indicating \nlimited instability with CAPE < 500 J/Kg from around Albany \nnorth and west. However, with increasing 0-6 km shear of 35-45 \nkt a few storms could produce gusty winds. It is likely storms \nwill not grow tall enough to become severe though. SPC continues\nto indicate a Marginal Risk (level 1 of 5) of severe storms for\na small area including the Mohawk Valley and SW Adirondacks for\na few possible rogue stronger gusts. Severe probs remain low \nthough due to limited instability and storms moving in primarily\nafter dark. Deep layer moisture does increase fairly \nsubstantially, with PWAT anomalies now forecast to be +2 to +3 \nSTDEV from the NAEFS. So brief torrential downpours could occur \nwith any convective elements, but the overall threat for flash \nflooding is low due to expected fast storm motion and dry \nantecedent conditions. Rainfall amounts will be generally less \nthan a half inch, except > 1\" in the W/SW Adirondacks due to \nupslope enhancement. Chances for showers will wane by daybreak \nas the cold front moves through.\n\nWith strengthening SW winds aloft later today into tonight \n(35-45 kt at 850 mb), occasional gusts of 20-30 mph will occur \nin favored S-N oriented valleys and southern facing higher \nterrain slopes. As the cold front pushes through early Thu \nmorning, winds will shift to the W-NW with more widespread gusts\nof 20-30 mph expected during the daylight hours Thu in a \ndeveloping deeper mixing environment. Temperatures will still \nbe warm (highs 80-85F in Hudson Valley) due to deeper mixing, \nalthough humidity levels will start to drop from north to south \nlater in the day. Dry conditions are expected through the day.\n\nKEY MESSAGE 2...\n\nAfter a seasonably cool and dry Fri-Sat period, temperatures \nwill warm above normal Sun into early next week as high pressure\nmoves off the coast and a warm southerly flow develops. It will\nalso become more humid during this time. Isolated/scattered \nshowers/T-storms may occur, especially on Tue as a cold front \nis expected to move east across the region.",
